# Software Engineering Guidelines

## 1. Core Principles

These principles guide all decisions. When in doubt, follow them in order.

1. Consistency over preference
2. Simplicity over cleverness
3. Reuse over duplication (when it does not introduce unnecessary complexity)
4. Data-driven over conditional logic
5. Clear structure over speed

---

## 2. Architecture & Structure

### 2.1 Separation of Responsibilities

- Business logic must not be mixed with infrastructure (DB, API, UI)
- Always isolate logic into dedicated functions, methods, or classes
- Avoid placing decision logic in controllers or transport layers

### 2.2 Orchestration Pattern

- Use **orchestrators** to coordinate smaller functions and methods
- Orchestrators should call smaller, focused units and not contain heavy logic themselves
- It is acceptable to have orchestrators calling other orchestrators

### 2.3 Avoid Tight Coupling

- Avoid creating unnecessary dependencies between modules
- Prefer composition over direct dependency
- If a dependency is required, isolate it through an adapter

### 2.4 Sub-Orchestrators (Reusable Flows)

- When multiple orchestrators reuse the same sequence of functions, evaluate grouping them into a **sub-orchestrator**
- Sub-orchestrators must represent a clear responsibility and remain composable

### 2.5 Data Fetching & Execution Strategy

- Always prioritize executing database logic in a **single call** when possible
- If multiple independent calls are required, execute them **simultaneously** (Promise.all or equivalent)
- Never execute independent operations sequentially without reason
- Performance takes priority over strict layering or reuse

---

## 3. Coding Guidelines

### 3.1 Reuse First

- Before creating new code, always check if similar logic already exists
- If reuse introduces additional complexity or affects multiple areas, explain the trade-offs and ask for confirmation
- Prefer reuse when it keeps the system simple and consistent

### 3.2 Function & Method Design

- Functions must be small and focused on a single responsibility
- Prefer composition of small units over large ones

### 3.3 Inputs & Outputs

- Keep inputs and outputs minimal and explicit
- Avoid optional arguments when possible; prefer extending interfaces

### 3.4 Variables & Mutations

- Always declare variables before mutating them
- Avoid inline mutations that make debugging difficult

### 3.5 Data-Driven Approach

- Avoid conditionals controlling business logic when possible
- Prefer: data structures, mappings, polymorphism

### 3.6 Abstraction Discipline

- If a use case calls an abstract function, encapsulate that call in a dedicated function
- Do not expose raw abstractions directly in business logic

---

## 4. Code Hygiene

### 4.1 Cleanup

- When removing or changing code: remove unused requests, clean unused variables, keep memory usage clean

### 4.2 Versioning

- Bump version numbers when meaningful changes are made

---

## 5. What We Avoid

- Large functions or methods
- Repeated code
- Optional arguments (when avoidable)
- Business logic in the wrong layer
- Direct database access without abstraction
- Creating new patterns without necessity
- Overuse of conditionals for business logic

---

## 6. Decision & Communication Rules

### 6.1 No Assumptions

- If the scope is unclear, always ask before proceeding

### 6.2 High-Impact Changes Require Confirmation

Before proceeding, **stop and ask** if the change may:
- Introduce new abstractions or shared logic
- Affect multiple parts of the system
- Change existing structure or patterns
- Introduce new dependencies

### 6.3 Scope Control

- Only implement what is requested
- Do not expand scope beyond the task

### 6.4 Consistency First

- Prefer extending existing structures over creating new ones

---

## 7. Paradigm & Design Approach

- Follow the existing paradigm and patterns used in the codebase
- Consider the most appropriate paradigm based on the problem
- Before applying a different paradigm, explain why and ask for confirmation
- Avoid mixing paradigms within the same scope without clear justification

---

## 8. Execution, Diffs, and Verification (AI-assisted work)

### 8.1 Disambiguation before implementation

- When more than one reasonable reading of the request exists, name the options—do not pick silently
- State explicit assumptions; confirm before coding if an assumption could change behavior

### 8.2 Surgical edits and change hygiene

- Prefer diffs where each changed line traces to the task
- Do not refactor or "improve" adjacent code unless required to complete the task
- Match existing local style even when it differs from your default
- Remove imports/variables your change made unused; do not treat pre-existing debt as in scope

### 8.3 Minimal new surface area

- Avoid new shared modules or generalized APIs for a one-off unless confirmed

### 8.4 Goal-driven verification

- Where useful, phrase work as checkable outcomes
- For multi-step work, a short plan with a verify note per step reduces rework
